{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001's causal mechanism for distinguishing independence is underspecified and potentially circular. The target asserts that if severing a suspected shared operator-level factor fails to alter the joint failure rate, independence is 'real.' But the mechanism provides no causal criterion for ruling out the possibility that the intervention simply missed the relevant shared cause — e.g., a common hardware vendor or a shared CDNs at a layer below the perturbed factor. Per the problem's demand, evidence must distinguish truly independent failures from failures that merely appear uncorrelated until a shared cause is found. 'The perturbation did not move the joint rate' is compatible with the shared cause being orthogonal to the perturbed factor; it does not causally establish independence. The claim thus conflates non-causal confounding with causal confirmation without specifying the mechanism for closing the search over shared causes.","counterexample":["A decentralized network whose services all share a common cloud-provider failure mode at a layer not perturbed by the operator-level intervention. Severing an operator-level shared factor (e.g., deployment tooling) produces no change in the joint failure rate, which the target would classify as 'real independence,' even though the cloud-provider shared cause remains and would produce correlated failure under a provider-side outage."],"target_alias":"SRC_001"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002's causal mechanism overgeneralizes: it equates 'can communicate' with a single shared consensus mechanism that constitutes a correlated failure point. But a shared protocol standard does not entail a shared implementation; mutually interoperable nodes using diverse protocol implementations (e.g., different TLS stacks, different DNS resolvers) can communicate without a single bug or zero-day binding all of them. The target conflates interoperability with implementation monoculture, so its causal mechanism for why independence is 'almost always an illusion' does not follow from its own premise about shared protocols.","counterexample":["A decentralized network using the same protocol standard (e.g., HTTP/TLS) but with independently developed, heterogeneous implementations across nodes. A bug in one implementation (e.g., a specific TLS library) does not constitute a correlated failure point for nodes using different implementations, even though they communicate via the shared standard."],"target_alias":"SRC_002"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_003's claimed causal mechanism is that common-cause traffic shocks exhaust small services' recovery resources while a large service's redundant pools absorb them, but this conflates a single correlated cause (common-cause traffic) with the full joint failure distribution. The problem asks whether a decentralized network is more resilient to correlated failure overall, not whether it is more resilient to one specific correlated failure mode. SRC_003 restricts its mechanism to traffic-spike/cascading-load scenarios (its counterconditions even concede resilience if shocks are truly independent), so it does not establish the general claim that the network is 'often less resilient to correlated failure' — it only identifies one causal channel. A traffic-spike channel is a candidate mechanism, not a demonstrated general one.","counterexample":["A decentralized network with heterogeneous rate-limiting, caching, and load-shedding policies (different vendors, different personnel) across its small services faces a correlated dependency-vulnerability shock (e.g., a shared library CVE). The large service's redundant but monoculture pools fail together due to the shared vulnerability, while the heterogeneous small services fail independently. Under this correlated failure mode, SRC_003's traffic-spike mechanism does not apply, yet it is a correlated failure the problem's scope covers."],"target_alias":"SRC_003"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_004's causal mechanism inverts the relevant logic. Lower entropy in the distribution of operator-level factors means less diversity, which should increase correlated failure risk, not decrease it; yet the target claims the decentralized network is more resilient if its operator-level causal entropy is strictly *lower* than the large service's. The mechanism states 'total redundancy is irrelevant if monoculture,' which implies the relevant driver is diversity (higher entropy), so the biconditional 'more resilient iff strictly lower entropy' contradicts its own stated causal rationale. Without resolving this inversion, the target does not supply a coherent causal mechanism linking operator-level diversity to resilience.","counterexample":["Two otherwise identical systems: System A has operator-level factor distribution with entropy H=1 bit (two equally weighted deployment philosophies); System B has H=2 bits (four equally weighted deployment philosophies). Per SRC_004's claim, System A (lower entropy) is more resilient than System B (higher entropy). But a shared-cause shock targeting one deployment philosophy affects half of System A and only a quarter of System B, so the higher-entropy System B is more resilient — contradicting the 'lower entropy is more resilient' condition."],"target_alias":"SRC_004"}]}